owning this is interesting your champion  women as part of your foundation because  obviously you've had an illustrious  career you're at the top of your game  but it's still very difficult for the  majority of women to follow that kind of  path it seems absolutely and  particularly worldwide when you look at  the opportunity swimming they are less  than the opportunities for men and it's  for a number of reasons firstly across  the world women don't get the same  educational opportunities as men so  that's the first start they don't get  the knowledge on top of that women tend  to not have the access to the capital to  the network's to the support networks  that they need and women often have lack  confidence why do they like confidence  because society is telling them this is  not what women do and therefore to  actually set stand up and say no this is  what I do and this is why I want to  succeed goes against that culture it is  interesting we're building up to  International Women's Day and when you  look out there at the moment the role  models are there we've got a female  prime minister we've got women heading  up the police force the army the UK  Supreme Court we've got a female doctor  who then in the papers today we've got  the first-ever female pilot at British  Airways coming out and saying that  across the airline industry women aren't  supported when it comes to maternity  allowance and support to help them get  back to work so it's obvious that you  know you have these female role models  but the steps aren't in place yet for  other women to get there  well role models are important because  first of all if you never actually see a  successful woman politician then you're  less likely as a young girl to think  that can be me once you do see that  women can make it to the top it really  does make a difference but of course if  it's always seen as these women are the  exceptions it's always seen that these  are the women who have had to sacrifice  other parts of their lives in order to  get there then you're not really  creating a level playing field so  whether it's issues about maternity pay  or maternity leave or whether it's a  more general question of whether women  are getting a fair chance in the world  it's really important that we see a  commitment a commitment by everybody to  make this happen  and that's why this partnership that my  foundation is having with Avon and their  stand for her campaign is so important  because Avon is a as a brand which is  about cosmetics obviously which is  something about women use cosmetics to  help their confidence it's a very  important part of that but it's also a  brand that reaches over a hundred  million women a year so by reaching out  to them and helping them in their  entrepreneurship journey by giving them  access to training and support we can  really shift the needle what was your  journey like because it's interesting  there you talk about the attitude of all  women can't do that and when you started  out in your legal career back in the 70s  I would imagine that was much more  prevalent than it is now what was your  journey like and particularly the legal  profession do you still see that  attitude around well sadly I think it's  true that though we find many more women  going into the legal profession when I  was it went into the legal profession it  was the first year to God above 10% now  it's you know roughly 50/50 going in but  when you look at how women progress  through the profession it's still the  case that women are dropping out and  we're still seeing issues for example  like sexual harassment and and bullying  within the workplace then women tend to  suffer from that it's interesting isn't  it you obviously had battles to fight in  your legal career but you were also  thrust into public life as the wife of  the Prime Minister Tony Blair and just  looking back at some of the coverage of  you during your time at number 10 with  your husband  there was this undertone in fact quite  often it was an undertone of a sexist  attitude towards you in terms of you  were a career woman whose husband just  happened to be Prime Minister so you  know there was newspaper coverage that  was almost like the cheek of it that she  has a career while her husband who's  Prime Minister or how dare she have  views on things you know when her  husband is Prime Minister how did she  deal with that because that can be the  attitude sometimes when it comes comes  to women in public life well sure I mean  you know that's why I called my book  speaking for myself because you know you  say of course I'm entitled to have views  but when your husband's the Prime  Minister it's you have to be careful how  you express those views because you're  not a  sensitive of the government and if you  want to be representative of the  government you should stand for election  yourself  I mean complaining about the press is  like complaining about the British  whether it was just that it was just  that underlying attitude of she's a  career woman you know and it's almost  like they'd begrudged you that because  you had a family you had a husband in a  very important role I always took the  view that just because my husband had  changed his job when gone into number  ten that didn't mean I should give up my  career because I had spent a long time  building up my career and become a QC  before my husband became a prime  minister yeah and there was a kind of  assumption when I first went in from the  system itself from Downing Street itself  that surely I would give up my job so  they were slightly surprised when I said  well no I want to continue with my job  because it's something that belongs to  me  and since your new husband left number  ten I mean obviously we talked about the  role of women we've touched on the role  of women in the legal system but also in  politics as well and there's been this  whole me too movement and some very  uncomfortable findings within  westminster there are people say haven't  been properly addressed what have you  made of a culture that it's sort of been  peeled back and seen what's going on  underneath and the public oh actually  quite disappointed with attitudes in  westminster in the treatment of women i  don't know well i'm sure the public are  disappointed but i'm not sure they're  entirely surprised because I think those  attitudes towards women whether it's  just about bossy females rather than you  know confident men the this is an  underlying trend in society what we're  talking about is allowing women the  opportunity as 50% of the population to  have equal access to all levels of  society and equal chances with men and  the truth is across the world there is  no country where that actually happens  and in some countries in the world for  example there are still countries in the  world where women can't even work or  leave the country without the permission  of their male relatives and you know  that is not treating women as equal  actors with equal dignity to men so we  have to do something about this idea  that somehow women are the inferior sex  it's interesting isn't it because we  look at government  and the political system as setting the  tone for these changes and there's a lot  of talk at the moment about the  political system being broken I mean  we've seen this breakaway group of MPs  a lot of people saying you know  government isn't fit for purpose when it  comes to tackling gender equality sexism  if this is you know held up as the  example of the country particularly with  gender inequality the treatment of  female politicians like Hannah Seabury  Diane Abbott  Luciana burger so you do wonder if  change can be effective effective if  that is the model that people are  looking at at the moment people think  it's broken well but if you look at the  figures again about where are women  getting equality across the world the  World Economic Forum do this report  about the global gender gap and they  look at four areas and you see in health  and education the gap is narrowing but  if you look at the areas where if you  like the power is which is economic  activity and political activity then the  gap is much bigger in economic  opportunity that gap between men and  women is like 64 women per 100 men and  for politics it's in the low 20s so it's  still showing that when you look at  where power is situated in the world  women are not getting equal access to  that and if we look at youth today and  the importance of ensuring that our  youth have the opportunities Klan  International just on a report where  they estimate that there are six hundred  and twenty eight million young people  between 16 and 25 across the world who  are not in education and don't have  access to employment and if you look and  break down that figure a little more you  discover that the vast majority of those  young girls young women who are not  getting that first step on the education  rather and then I'm not being enabled to  express themselves to find themselves to  go on a journey to be able to support  themselves and that's why  entrepreneurship is one of the keys to  this because if we can help more women  entrepreneurs come into the economy so  that they can devise their businesses  suit themselves that can suit the way  they work so that they can combine their  work with their family obligations then  we will not only increase the world a  gross domestic product which is a good  thing in itself but even more important  will enable women to take their rightful  place in society so that they are  respected that their voices are heard  and they can stand up to things like  bullying sexual violence or just the  complete neglect of women's issues
 

